\begin{wrapfigure}{r}{0.34\textwidth}
\centering
\begin{tikzpicture}[
  stage/.style={draw=blue!60!black, rounded corners=3pt, fill=blue!5, text width=3.4cm, align=center, font=\small, inner sep=5pt},
  arr/.style={-{Stealth}, thick, draw=black!70}]
  \node[stage] (p) at (0,0) {\textbf{Real decision problem}\\ \scriptsize what to make, ship, schedule};
  \node[stage, below=0.45cm of p] (m) {\textbf{Mathematical model}\\ $\max\; \c^\top\x$ s.t. $A\x \le \b$};
  \node[stage, below=0.45cm of m] (sv) {\textbf{Solver}};
  \node[stage, below=0.45cm of sv, fill=green!6, draw=green!50!black] (d) {\textbf{Decision + guarantee}\\ \scriptsize a plan, and how good it is};
  \draw[arr] (p) -- (m);
  \draw[arr] (m) -- (sv);
  \draw[arr] (sv) -- (d);
\end{tikzpicture}
\caption{The mathematical programming workflow.}
\label{fig:mp-pipeline}
\end{wrapfigure}
At the heart of Mathematical Programming is the objective function: a mathematical expression that defines the criterion to be optimized, such as minimizing costs or maximizing profits. The decision variables represent the choices available, and the constraints reflect the limitations or requirements of the problem, such as resource capacities or regulatory conditions.

