source_text:
\begin{solution}(Exercise~\ref{ex:graphical-method-one})
The feasible region has vertices $(0,0)$, $(3,0)$, $(3,1)$, and $(0,4)$. Evaluating the objective: $z(0,0)=0$, $z(3,0)=9$, $z(3,1)=11$, and $z(0,4)=8$. Sweeping the level curves of $z = 3x+2y$ in the direction of the gradient $(3,2)$, the last level curve to touch the region passes through $(3,1)$, where the constraints $x+y\leq 4$ and $x\leq 3$ are both binding. The optimal solution is $(x^*,y^*) = (3,1)$ with optimal value $z^* = 11$.
\end{solution}

target_text:
\begin{solution}(Latihan~\ref{ex:graphical-method-one})
Daerah layak memiliki titik-titik ekstrem $(0,0)$, $(3,0)$, $(3,1)$, dan $(0,4)$. Evaluasi objektif memberikan $z(0,0)=0$, $z(3,0)=9$, $z(3,1)=11$, dan $z(0,4)=8$. Dengan menyapukan kurva aras $z = 3x+2y$ ke arah gradien $(3,2)$, kurva aras terakhir yang menyentuh daerah tersebut melewati $(3,1)$, tempat kendala $x+y\leq 4$ dan $x\leq 3$ sama-sama aktif. Solusi optimalnya adalah $(x^*,y^*) = (3,1)$ dengan nilai optimal $z^* = 11$.
\end{solution}

source_text:
\begin{solution}(Exercise~\ref{ex:infeasible})
In fact, constraints 2 and 3 point away from each other, and hence there are no feasible solutions.
\end{solution}

target_text:
\begin{solution}(Latihan~\ref{ex:infeasible})
Kenyataannya, kendala 2 dan 3 menunjuk ke arah yang saling menjauh sehingga tidak ada solusi layak.
\end{solution}

