\begin{ex}{Reading a mid-solve tableau}{}
\label{ex:tableau-read-mid}
\stars{1} The tableau below is the second tableau of this chapter's running example, reached after one pivot:
\begin{center}
\renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.3}
\begin{tabular}{c|ZZZZZ|Z}
\toprule
Basis & $x$ & $y$ & $s_1$ & $s_2$ & $s_3$ & RHS\\
\midrule
$z$   & $-\tfrac12$ & $0$ & $0$ & $0$ & $\tfrac32$ & $21$\\
\midrule
$s_1$ & $\tfrac12$ & $0$ & $1$ & $0$ & $-\tfrac12$ & $2$\\
$s_2$ & $\tfrac32$ & $0$ & $0$ & $1$ & $-\tfrac12$ & $9$\\
$y$   & $\tfrac12$ & $1$ & $0$ & $0$ & $\tfrac12$ & $7$\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Which variables are basic and which are nonbasic?
    \item Read off the basic feasible solution and the objective value.
    \item What are the reduced costs of the nonbasic variables?
    \item Is this tableau optimal?  If not, which variable enters next?
\end{enumerate}
\exrefs{\S\ref{sec:tableau-building}, first worked tableau; \S\ref{sec:tableau-pivot}}
\end{ex}

